.TH MV 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
mv \- move or rename files and directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mv
.RB [ \-finTv ]
.I source dest
.br
.B mv
.RB [ \-finv ]
.IR source ...\&
.I directory
.br
.B mv
.RB [ \-finv ]
.BI "\-t " directory
.IR source ...\&
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mv
renames a file or directory, or moves files and directories into a
directory.
It is the
.BR busybox (1)
applet, which has fewer options than GNU
.BR mv ;
see
.BR NOTES .
.SS Operands
With
.BR \-t ,
every operand is a
.I source
and is moved into
.IR directory .
Otherwise, with two operands,
.I source
is renamed to
.I dest
itself, except that when
.I dest
is an existing directory, or a symbolic link or junction to one,
.I source
is moved into it.
.B \-T
always renames to
.IR dest .
With more than two operands, the last is
.IR directory .
With no operand, or with one operand and no
.BR \-t ,
.B mv
writes a usage message on standard error and exits with status 1.
.PP
A move into
.I directory
is named after the last component of
.IR source ,
with trailing slashes and backslashes removed, so
.B mv src/ out
makes
.I out/src
when
.I out
exists.
.B mv
does not check beforehand that
.I directory
exists or is a directory: the move of each
.I source
then fails on its own, with a reason such as
.B No such file or directory
or
.BR "Invalid argument" .
.SS What is moved
A move is a rename, not a copy: the file or directory keeps its
contents, times, and attributes.
A directory is moved with everything in it, and cannot be moved into
itself, which fails with
.BR "Invalid argument" .
A symbolic link or junction is moved as a link, and its target is not
touched.
For moves to another volume, see
.BR NOTES .
.SS Existing destinations
.B \-n
skips a source whose destination exists.
Otherwise, unless
.B \-f
is given,
.B mv
asks before replacing an existing destination if
.B \-i
is given, or if the destination is read-only and standard input is a
console.
It writes
.BI "mv: overwrite \(aq" dest "\(aq?\&"
on standard error and reads a line from standard input.
The destination is replaced only if the first character of the line
that is not a space or tab is
.B y
or
.BR Y ,
and otherwise the source is skipped, which is not an error.
End of input counts as no.
.PP
An existing destination file is replaced by the moved file, even if it
is read-only.
The moved file keeps its own attributes, so the replacement is writable
if the moved file is.
An existing destination directory is never replaced: moving a file or
directory onto one fails with
.BR "Is a directory" ,
even when it is empty.
A directory moved onto an existing file does replace it.
.SS Output
.B mv
reports each error on standard error as
.BI "mv: " message
and goes on with the remaining operands.
A source that cannot be moved is reported as
.BI "mv: can\(aqt rename \(aq" source "\(aq: " reason \c
.RI .
Prompts go to standard error.
Only
.B \-v
and
.B \-\-help
write to standard output.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-fv ,
and may follow operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
The argument of
.B \-t
may be attached, as in
.BR \-tbin .
A long option may be abbreviated to any unambiguous prefix, and takes
its argument after
.B =
or as the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Do not ask before replacing an existing destination, even a read-only
one.
Of
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-i ,
and
.BR \-n ,
only the last given takes effect.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Ask before replacing any existing destination, as described above.
Only the last of
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-i ,
and
.B \-n
takes effect.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-clobber
Skip, without a message, each source whose destination exists.
A destination that differs from the source only in case exists, so
.B mv \-n a A
does nothing.
Only the last of
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-i ,
and
.B \-n
takes effect.
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-no\-target\-directory
Rename to
.I dest
itself, never into it.
Requires exactly two operands, and is incompatible with
.BR \-t .
If
.I dest
is an existing directory, the move fails: a
.I source
that is not a directory is refused with
.BI "mv: \(aq" dest "\(aq is a directory"
and exit status 1, and a directory cannot replace a directory.
.TP
\fB\-t\fR \fIdirectory\fR, \fB\-\-target\-directory\fR=\fIdirectory\fR
Move every operand into
.IR directory .
Incompatible with
.BR \-T .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Write
.BI \(aq source "\(aq \-> \(aq" dest \(aq
on standard output for each source, after trying to move it.
The line is written whether the move succeeded, failed, or was skipped
by
.B \-n
or by an answer to
.BR \-i ,
but not when
.B \-T
refuses it.
.I dest
is the name of the destination itself, or, for a move into a
directory, the directory and the new name joined with a slash.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument only, write a usage message on standard output
and exit with status 0.
The message omits the long options and
.BR \-v .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments are expanded when busybox starts, for a caller
such as
.B cmd.exe
that does not expand them.
When it is unset, busybox sets it to
.B 0
for the programs it starts, so commands run from the kit's shell get the
shell's expansion only.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.PP
Messages written to a console also depend on
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ,
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_ALL ,
and
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
and Windows error dialogs on
.BR BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS ,
as for every busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including sources skipped by
.B \-n
or an answer to
.BR \-i ,
and a name moved onto itself.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid command line, with a usage message on standard error, or any
operand that could not be moved.
.SH EXAMPLES
Rename a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ mv old.c new.c
.EE
.RE
.PP
Move the C sources into a directory, listing each move:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ mv \-v *.c src
\(aqhello.c\(aq \-> \(aqsrc/hello.c\(aq
\(aqmain.c\(aq \-> \(aqsrc/main.c\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Move only the files that are not already in the destination:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ mv \-n *.txt backup
.EE
.RE
.PP
Change only the case of a name:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ mv readme.txt README.TXT
.EE
.RE
.PP
Move the files that another command lists into a directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find build \-name \(aq*.o\(aq | xargs mv \-t obj
.EE
.RE
.PP
Move a file to a directory on another drive:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ mv report.txt d:/archive
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR mv :
.IP \(bu 2
.B mv
does not detect that source and destination are the same file.
A name moved onto itself is left alone, and a name moved onto another
hard link to the same file is removed, leaving the other name.
.IP \(bu 2
An existing directory is never replaced, not even an empty one.
.IP \(bu 2
A directory replaces an existing file.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-v
writes a line for every source, including those that failed or were
skipped.
.IP \(bu 2
A destination directory that is missing, or is not a directory, is not
detected before moving starts.
.IP \(bu 2
A trailing slash on a
.I dest
that does not exist is ignored, so
.B mv f new/
renames the file
.I f
to
.IR new .
.IP \(bu 2
Options not listed above, such as
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-S ,
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-Z ,
.BR \-\-backup ,
.BR \-\-suffix ,
.BR \-\-update ,
and
.BR \-\-strip\-trailing\-slashes ,
are invalid.
.PP
On a case-insensitive file system, a destination that differs from its
source only in case exists, so
.B \-n
skips such a move and
.B \-i
asks first.
Otherwise the move changes the case of the name.
.PP
A file moved to another volume is copied and deleted by Windows, and
keeps its times and read-only attribute.
A directory or junction cannot be moved to another volume: the move
fails with
.B Permission denied
and the source is left alone.
Copy a directory with
.B cp \-a
and remove the original with
.B rm \-r
instead.
Replacing a read-only file on another volume also fails with
.BR "Permission denied" .
.B mv
has its own fallback that copies a tree and removes the original, but
it runs only when the rename fails with a cross-device error (EXDEV),
which none of these moves produce.
.PP
Windows also refuses to move a directory that another process uses as
its current directory, with
.BR "Permission denied" .
.PP
Paths may have drive letters and backslashes.
Names in a directory are joined with
.BR / ,
so
.B mv \-v x \(aqc:\etools\(aq
writes names such as
.IR c:\etools/x .
A bare drive such as
.I d:
names the current directory of that drive, so
.B mv x d:
writes
.IR d:x .
Names may contain any Unicode character on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
A full path of 260 or more characters, counting the current directory
for a relative path, fails with
.BR "No such file or directory" .
.PP
From
.BR cmd.exe ,
.B mv
expands wildcards in its arguments itself; see
.BR BB_GLOBBING .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR cp (1),
.BR ln (1),
.BR rm (1)
